            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Computes x times the natural log of y, element by element - and gives 0 whenever x is 0, even where log y is minus infinity.\n\nIt is the safe way to write the terms of an entropy or cross-entropy (a probability times the log of a probability), where zero probabilities are normal and a hand-written x * log y would turn them into NaN that then spoils every sum after it.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT JUST A MULTIPLY:\nlog(0) is minus infinity, and 0 times minus infinity is undefined, so x * log(y) gives NaN (not a number) at x = 0, y = 0. The limit is 0, and 0 is the answer you want. This node returns it: whenever x is 0 the result is 0, whatever y is - unless y is itself NaN.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.xlogy\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor 1 or number in:\nx, the multiplier - a tensor, or a single number applied to every y. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\ntensor 2 or number in:\ny, the value whose log is taken - a tensor, or a single number. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it BEFORE x. It is converted to x's dtype and device. The two are matched element by element, or broadcast when one is a single value.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, in the broadcast shape of the two inputs.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nWith x not 0: y = 0 gives minus infinity (for positive x) and negative y gives NaN, as log does.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second; it makes 200 values of p from 0 to 1. 't array array' sends them to the second input and then the first (right outlet first), so the node computes p log p. The lower plot is that, from -0.4 (bottom) to 0.05 (top): a dip to -0.368 at p = 0.368 (1/e), and exactly 0 at BOTH ends - at p = 1 because log 1 is 0, and at p = 0 because of the protection; written by hand that first point would be NaN. Minus the sum of these over a distribution is its entropy. Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look elsewhere (negative values give NaN, which the plot leaves out).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.xlog1py is the same with log(1 + y). t.special.entr computes minus p log p in one step. t.special.logits is the other place a log near 0 and 1 needs care.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 825,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Computes x times the natural log of y, element by element - and gives 0 whenever x is 0, even where log y is minus infinity.\n\nIt is the safe way to write the terms of an entropy or cross-entropy (a probability times the log of a probability), where zero probabilities are normal and a hand-written x * log y would turn them into NaN that then spoils every sum after it.\n\nWHY IT IS NOT JUST A MULTIPLY:\nlog(0) is minus infinity, and 0 times minus infinity is undefined, so x * log(y) gives NaN (not a number) at x = 0, y = 0. The limit is 0, and 0 is the answer you want. This node returns it: whenever x is 0 the result is 0, whatever y is - unless y is itself NaN.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.special.xlogy\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor 1 or number in:\nx, the multiplier - a tensor, or a single number applied to every y. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\ntensor 2 or number in:\ny, the value whose log is taken - a tensor, or a single number. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it BEFORE x. It is converted to x's dtype and device. The two are matched element by element, or broadcast when one is a single value.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ntensor out:\nThe result, in the broadcast shape of the two inputs.\n\nWHERE IT IS DEFINED:\nWith x not 0: y = 0 gives minus infinity (for positive x) and negative y gives NaN, as log does.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe metro re-clicks t.linspace ten times a second; it makes 200 values of p from 0 to 1. 't array array' sends them to the second input and then the first (right outlet first), so the node computes p log p. The lower plot is that, from -0.4 (bottom) to 0.05 (top): a dip to -0.368 at p = 0.368 (1/e), and exactly 0 at BOTH ends - at p = 1 because log 1 is 0, and at p = 0 because of the protection; written by hand that first point would be NaN. Minus the sum of these over a distribution is its entropy. Drag start and stop on t.linspace to look elsewhere (negative values give NaN, which the plot leaves out).\n\nRELATED:\nt.special.xlog1py is the same with log(1 + y). t.special.entr computes minus p log p in one step. t.special.logits is the other place a log near 0 and 1 needs care."
        },
